  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nA light shower or two persists across southwest Minnesota this \nafternoon. These showers remain ahead of a cold front that is just \npushing into the forecast area. This front will serve as the focal \npoint for potential severe storm development later this afternoon. \nThe main shortwave is just north and east of the forecast area. \nHowever, the upper level jet will be nosing its exit region into the \nsouthwestern half of the forecast area. This presents a bit of a \ndisplacement in where the better shear and forcing will lie. Some \ncapping remains across the area but the weakest amount of capping \nlooks to be just along the front itself. Think that 1 to 4 storms is \npossible this afternoon and evening IF storms are able to develop. \nIF storms develop, more than sufficient instability will be in place \nwith CAPE values up to 2,000 J/kg to 2,500 J/kg as moisture pools \nahead of the front. With part of the upper level jet overhead, \nvertical shear will also be sufficient with deep layer shear values \nbetween 40 to 50 knots. DCAPE values will be highest along and just \nbehind the front with magnitudes up to 1,200 J/kg. Thus, Large hail \nto 2+ inches and damaging winds to 50-70 mph will be the main \nhazards. A low 2% chance exists for a tornado as the low level jet \nbegins to strengthen this evening, slightly expanding low level \nhodographs and increasing 0-3km storm relative helicity (SRH) values \nup to 100+ m2/s2. Any chance for severe storms for be waning through \nthe evening hours as nocturnal stabilization increases capping. The \npreviously mentioned cold front will stall out over the forecast \narea tonight, leaving muggy conditions in place. With light winds \nexpected, patchy fog is once again possible. \n\nHighs will remain in the 80s across the area on Friday. The \npreviously mentioned cold front will push through the forecast area \nand end up right along the Missouri River. This could result in a \nfew storms developing on this boundary before quickly pushing off to \nthe south and east. However, latest LREF probabilities remain low \n(20-30%) for a tenth of an inch of rain or more. The HREF shows \nlittle to no probabilities for any precipitation. This is in line \nwith the latest hi-res guidance as the bulk of this guidance shows \nstorms developing south of the surface front in Nebraska. Will keep \nan eye on this potential in case storms trend back north, but for \nnow, it looks most likely that the forecast area will remain dry \ntomorrow. Low temperatures will fall to the upper 50s and low 60s \novernight. \n\nSaturday will be a dry day as high pressure passes through the \nforecast area. High temperatures will be a little cooler, only \nwarming to the upper 70s to low to mid 80s. The dry conditions will \nbe brief as the next round of rain will return on Sunday as another \nshortwave trough will push into the Northern Plains. Latest \nensembles show a 40-70% chance for rainfall totals to exceed a tenth \nof an inch through the day. Latest machine learning (ML) guidance \nkeeps the highest chances for severe storms to the west of the \nforecast area. This may change over the next few days so will \ncontinue to monitor the potential for severe storms on Sunday.\n\nHigh temperatures will remain near seasonable in the upper 70s and \n80s for the first half of next week. Active zonal to northwest flow \nwill persist aloft as well, contributing to additional risks for \nshowers and storms. ML guidance does shows a small increase in \npotential severe storm risk, though the probabilities only 5 to 10%. \nSomething to keep an eye on going forwards as weak shortwaves within \nthe active flow can deliver additional chances for potential severe \nstorms.",
